DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aattooll() function converts the initial portion of the string pointed to
     by _n_p_t_r to _l_o_n_g integer representation.

     It is equivalent to:

           strtol(nptr, (char **)NULL, 10);

     The aattoollll() function converts the initial portion of the string pointed
     to by _n_p_t_r to _l_o_n_g _l_o_n_g integer representation.

     It is equivalent to:

           strtoll(nptr, (char **)NULL, 10);

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     The FreeBSD implementations of the aattooll() and aattoollll() functions are thin
     wrappers around ssttrrttooll() and ssttrrttoollll() respectively, so these functions
     will affect the value of _e_r_r_n_o in the same way that the ssttrrttooll() and
     ssttrrttoollll() functions are able to.  This behavior of aattooll() and aattoollll() is
     not required by ISO/IEC 9899:1990 (“ISO C90”) or ISO/IEC 9899:1999
     (“ISO C99”), but it is allowed by all of ISO/IEC 9899:1990 (“ISO C90”),
     ISO/IEC 9899:1999 (“ISO C99”) and IEEE Std 1003.1-2001 (“POSIX.1”).

EERRRROORRSS
     The functions aattooll() and aattoollll() may affect the value of _e_r_r_n_o on an er‐
     ror.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     atof(3), atoi(3), strtod(3), strtol(3), strtoul(3)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
     The aattooll() function conforms to ISO/IEC 9899:1990 (“ISO C90”).  The
     aattoollll() function conforms to ISO/IEC 9899:1999 (“ISO C99”).

